      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 12.07,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": true,
      "char_count": 79,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p15_b18",
      "text": "finite simplex this term is perfectly well defined, but in RLHF it is not directly observable:\nthe response space is too large to enumerate, and a rollout group shows only a small\nrandom subset of possible completions. Estimating the maximum by taking the largest\nvalue inside the sampled group is therefore not faithful to the target in general, because\nthe response attaining the true maximum over the hidden simplex may simply not have\nbeen sampled.\n",
      "bbox": [
        71.7,
        472.86,
        524.91,
        574.35
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        286,
        1891,
        2099,
        2297
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.84,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 452,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p15_b19",
      "text": "We therefore replace the exact maximum by a smooth log-sum-exp relaxation. The\npoint of this relaxation is not cosmetic: it converts the adversarial correction into an\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        580.25,
        523.36,
        610.23
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
